WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:06.800
Here's a mystery -- who exactly wants to see&nbsp;
another Sherlock Holmes movie? More than ten years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:12.320
after the last one, we're about to find out&nbsp;
-- and the answer probably won't be pretty.

00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:15.920
"I agree it's not my best&nbsp;
disguise, but I had to make do."

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:21.600
Ben Affleck may be a movie star, but the days of&nbsp;
an actor being able to put butts in seats on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:26.560
basis of their celebrity alone are pretty&nbsp;
much over… even for the star of "Gigli."

00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:28.800
Knowing this, Disney's release strategy for "Deep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:32.560
Water," a film produced under&nbsp;
its 20th Century Studios brand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:38.160
seems to be "release the movie quietly in&nbsp;
January, and whatever happens, happens."

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:41.200
In the film, Affleck plays a&nbsp;
wealthy husband hoping to stay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:46.640
far away from a pricey divorce by letting his&nbsp;
wife, played by Ana de Armas, have extramarital&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:52.320
affairs -- but he becomes suspect number one&nbsp;
when one of her paramours suddenly vanishes.

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:56.880
The movie comes out on January 14,&nbsp;
and as of the making of this video,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.880 --> 00:01:00.240
there's not a trailer, or even poster for it yet.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:05.520
With zero hype for the movie so far, "Deep&nbsp;
Water" seems to be in deep… well, you know.

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:11.520
Betting against Marvel is like betting against&nbsp;
the Harlem Globetrotters. But just because Marvel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:17.274
boasts Earth's Mightiest Heroes, that doesn't mean&nbsp;
the brand is invincible — especially when it's not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.274 --> 00:01:23.404
Marvel Studios, but Sony's Marvel-affiliated&nbsp;
Spider-Man spinoff series. Confused?

00:01:23.702 --> 00:01:26.632
"The Multiverse!"

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:29.920
It's hard to tell where Sony's&nbsp;
"Morbius" falls in the canon. We&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:33.280
think it's a spinoff of Tom&nbsp;
Hardy's "Venom" series, with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:37.360
nothing to do with the Marvel Cinematic&nbsp;
Universe -- but these days, who can tell?

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:39.920
"I… am… Venom."

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:47.360
What we do know is that thanks to lots of delays&nbsp;
and bad buzz, the anticipation for this movie is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:52.800
colder than its January release date, where&nbsp;
Sony is sending it to die a quiet undeath.

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:56.960
You may be saying, "So what? People said the&nbsp;
same thing about 'Venom.'" But Venom was one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.960 --> 00:02:03.280
of the 90s' most popular comic book villains,&nbsp;
and Morbius has always been D-list or lower.

00:02:03.280 --> 00:02:05.840
It's possible that "Morbius"&nbsp;
will surprise everyone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:12.200
but it definitely won't be a big shocker if&nbsp;
this vampire monster mash mostly just sucks.

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:17.360
Video game movies are on an upswing,&nbsp;
based on the recent successes of "Pokemon:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:21.040
Detective Pikachu," "Sonic the&nbsp;
Hedgehog," and "Mortal Kombat."

00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:24.960
Is this a sign of a reversing trend&nbsp;
for video game movies, one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:30.560
most critically maligned and commercially&nbsp;
spurious of all movie genres? Maybe -- but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:35.120
we're not betting on it, especially not for&nbsp;
the first big-screen-outing of Nathan Drake.

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:38.960
Yes, he's the main protagonist of&nbsp;
"Uncharted," one of the best-selling,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:44.320
most critically acclaimed video game series&nbsp;
ever -- but the key phrase here is "video game."

00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:47.040
Gamers like playing games as Nathan Drake,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:51.520
they don't necessarily like buying a ticket to&nbsp;
watch Tom Holland play a young Nathan Drake.

00:02:51.520 --> 00:02:56.960
While Tom Holland may be a draw as&nbsp;
Spider-Man, everybody is a draw as Spider-Man.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.960 --> 00:03:00.880
Outside the MCU, Holland hasn't shown&nbsp;
his mettle, and playing a square-jawed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:05.520
adventurer seems like a stretch for the&nbsp;
charmingly befuddled young Brit. Still,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:10.240
we're always up for an old-school adventure. We'll&nbsp;
just probably be the only people in the theater.

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:15.600
Marvel Studios could probably release a&nbsp;
movie about Doop and it would still gross&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:20.000
a billion dollars. Meanwhile, DC's&nbsp;
cinematic output has been a little more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:24.800
hit or miss. Joker grossed a billion&nbsp;
dollars; Birds of Prey, not so much.

00:03:24.800 --> 00:03:31.760
Now DC is taking a swing with "DC League of&nbsp;
Super-Pets," scheduled for summer 2022. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.760 --> 00:03:37.040
the animated film, Superman goes on vacation, so&nbsp;
protecting the planet is left to his dog Krypto,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:40.560
Ace the bat hound, and a league of super pets.

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:44.480
Krypto and Ace are voiced by&nbsp;
Dwayne Johnson and Kevin Hart,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:49.760
stars of the rebooted "Jumanji" series.&nbsp;
They're joined by a stacked voice cast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:53.120
including Keanu Reeves, Kate&nbsp;
McKinnon, and John Krasinski.

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:58.160
"The LEGO Batman Movie" showed you can make&nbsp;
a popular, family-friendly comedy spoofing DC&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.160 --> 00:04:04.880
characters. Still, this feels like a charming HBO&nbsp;
Max series, not a blockbuster movie families will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.880 --> 00:04:10.560
shell out money for, at least enough to justify&nbsp;
what we're sure is a budget pushing $100 million.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.120 --> 00:04:17.280
If it's good, it may make money. If it's so-so, it&nbsp;
may end up being more of a wait-and-stream thing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.280 --> 00:04:22.240
If it's bad, well… it wouldn't be the first&nbsp;
DC property to arrive to harsh criticism.

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:26.040
"I am not someone who is loved.”

00:04:27.040 --> 00:04:31.600
Roland Emmerich's schtick of "humans vs.&nbsp;
world-ending disaster" used to be a pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.600 --> 00:04:36.960
reliable formula, financially. But for more than&nbsp;
a decade, the biggest disasters in Emmerich's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:43.920
filmography haven't been on the screen but at&nbsp;
the box office. He hasn't had a hit since 2009's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:49.920
"2012," which predicted the end of the world but&nbsp;
actually foretold the end of his impressive run.

00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:54.240
In between ,there have been a few&nbsp;
misbegotten attempts at small-scale films,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:59.280
like "Anonymous" and "Stonewall", peppered&nbsp;
with massive bombs like "Independence Day:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.280 --> 00:05:04.240
Resurgence" and "Midway." In our prediction, look&nbsp;
for "Moonfall" to continue the downward trend.

00:05:04.240 --> 00:05:04.800
"You know, we are just inside the moon." "

00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:07.360
That might be the greatest&nbsp;
sentence anyone's ever said."

00:05:07.360 --> 00:05:11.360
As the title suggests, "Moonfall" is about&nbsp;
the moon getting knocked out of its orbit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.360 --> 00:05:15.840
and going on a collision course with Earth.&nbsp;
It stars Halle Berry and Patrick Wilson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:21.520
talented value-adds in "John Wick" and "Aquaman"&nbsp;
movies but not reliable draws on their own.

00:05:21.520 --> 00:05:28.240
So with an eye-rolling concept and unreliable box&nbsp;
office draws, "Moonfall" has a massive mountain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.240 --> 00:05:33.840
to climb to earn a profit on its reported $150&nbsp;
million budget. Maybe China will save the day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.840 --> 00:05:40.000
but domestically, the only audience for "Moonfall"&nbsp;
are dudes aged 18-to-34 who are watching it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:46.080
ironically. A prime demo, to be sure, but not&nbsp;
enough to save "Moonfall" from crashing to Earth.

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:53.280
Nearly 20 years after its original release,&nbsp;
"Shrek 2" remains one of the 15 biggest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.280 --> 00:05:58.160
animated worldwide hits ever and one of&nbsp;
the top five of all time domestically.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.160 --> 00:06:04.320
A big reason why was Antonio Banderas' turn as&nbsp;
the swashbuckling Spaniard cat, Puss in Boots.

00:06:04.320 --> 00:06:09.760
The character was so popular that he even inspired&nbsp;
his own spinoff, which was a big hit in 2011,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.760 --> 00:06:17.360
grossing $149 million domestically and $554&nbsp;
million worldwide on a $130 million budget.

00:06:17.360 --> 00:06:22.480
However, 11 years will have elapsed since&nbsp;
the original "Puss in Boots," and the magic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.480 --> 00:06:28.720
number between sequels is between two and four&nbsp;
years. Any shorter and you risk oversaturation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.720 --> 00:06:34.480
Any longer and you risk the audience moving on.&nbsp;
The latter is particularly a risk with animated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.480 --> 00:06:39.760
movies, as the original audience has literally&nbsp;
grown up. Disney and Pixar can get away with it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.760 --> 00:06:44.560
but we don't think "Puss in Boots" has that&nbsp;
sort of staying power. We're predicting the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.560 --> 00:06:49.680
box office for "Puss in Boots: The Last Wish"&nbsp;
will stink worse than week-old kitty litter.

00:06:50.720 --> 00:06:55.440
One of the biggest casualties of the COVID-19&nbsp;
pandemic's effect on the film industry may be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.440 --> 00:07:01.200
"Top Gun: Maverick," which is now set to arrive&nbsp;
36 years after the release of the original movie.&nbsp;

00:07:01.200 --> 00:07:04.480
Originally slated for release&nbsp;
in the holiday season of 2020,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.480 --> 00:07:08.640
it's now set to take off,&nbsp;
finally, in September 2022.

00:07:08.640 --> 00:07:12.560
As far as release date shuffling goes,&nbsp;
"Top Gun: Maverick" has been moved around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.560 --> 00:07:17.120
so much we feel like we're sitting in the&nbsp;
back of a F-16 fighter jet hitting Mach 2.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.120 --> 00:07:22.720
Is that disadvantage made up by the combo&nbsp;
of Tom Cruise and nostalgia? We doubt it.

00:07:22.720 --> 00:07:28.400
Cruise's four-decade movie star run is nearly&nbsp;
unprecedented in its level of success, but lately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.400 --> 00:07:32.640
he's only been reliable in the "Mission:&nbsp;
Impossible" movies. Maverick is one of his most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.640 --> 00:07:38.880
iconic characters, but "Top Gun" is memorable in&nbsp;
a "it was on TBS in the afternoons" kind of way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.880 --> 00:07:42.480
not in a "my family watched&nbsp;
it every year" kind of way.

00:07:42.480 --> 00:07:47.920
There's a difference between genuine nostalgia,&nbsp;
a la "Jurassic World," and a sequel to a popular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.920 --> 00:07:53.280
movie from a bygone era, like "Independence&nbsp;
Day: Resurgence". Decades later, the world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.280 --> 00:07:56.720
doesn't feel like it was begging for&nbsp;
a "Top Gun" sequel. In other words,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.720 --> 00:08:02.080
we just don't think modern moviegoers&nbsp;
still have the need... the need for speed.

00:08:03.200 --> 00:08:08.880
Elle Woods first sashayed her way onto Harvard&nbsp;
Law's campus for 2001's "Legally Blonde."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.880 --> 00:08:13.200
Fresh-faced Reese Witherspoon proved her&nbsp;
box office bona fides with the movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.200 --> 00:08:18.480
powering the $18 million film to&nbsp;
a $141 million worldwide haul.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.480 --> 00:08:23.600
You don't need a high-priced law degree to know&nbsp;
profit margins like that are mighty impressive.

00:08:23.600 --> 00:08:28.400
Two years later, "Legally Blonde 2: Red, White,&nbsp;
&amp; Blonde" brought Elle Woods to Washington, D.C.,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.400 --> 00:08:36.480
and put $90 million domestically and $125 million&nbsp;
into studio MGM's pocket. On a $25 million budget,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.480 --> 00:08:42.480
"Legally Blonde 2" was slightly less successful,&nbsp;
and that was the end of the story… until now.

00:08:42.480 --> 00:08:47.760
MGM famously had monetary woes throughout the&nbsp;
2000s, which probably contributed to putting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.760 --> 00:08:52.240
off a timely three-peat. In the meantime, the&nbsp;
"Legally Blonde" brand expanded beyond the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.240 --> 00:08:57.040
multiplex to Broadway. So, nearly 20 years&nbsp;
later, does the moviegoing audience still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.040 --> 00:09:02.080
care about "Legally Blonde," a franchise&nbsp;
very much of its time? We don't think so.

00:09:02.080 --> 00:09:06.800
While the plot is unknown, Elle Woods'&nbsp;
saga succeeded because it was the ultimate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.800 --> 00:09:09.920
underdog story -- and we're not&nbsp;
just talking about her chihuahua.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.920 --> 00:09:14.880
We imagine this movie will rely more on&nbsp;
nostalgia than on inspiring new audiences,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.880 --> 00:09:18.720
and in today's movie landscape,&nbsp;
that just won't be enough.

00:09:19.680 --> 00:09:20.188
"What?" "Elle."

00:09:23.760 --> 00:09:27.200
Have we passed peak Sherlock Holmes? Maybe.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.200 --> 00:09:33.120
Sir Arthur Conan Doyle's world-famous sleuth&nbsp;
first appeared in "A Study in Scarlet" in 1887,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.120 --> 00:09:36.880
and the character has remained a&nbsp;
figure in pop culture ever since.

00:09:36.880 --> 00:09:41.200
BBC's modern-day "Sherlock" series brought&nbsp;
Benedict Cumberbatch's "high-functioning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.200 --> 00:09:45.760
sociopath" Holmes to the small screen, and&nbsp;
Warner Brothers' "Sherlock Holmes" films,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.760 --> 00:09:48.640
which turned Robert Downey&nbsp;
Jr.'s Holmes and Jude Law's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.640 --> 00:09:52.560
John Watson into Victorian-era&nbsp;
action heroes on the big screen.

00:09:52.560 --> 00:09:58.720
Both 2009's "Sherlock Holmes" and 2011's "Sherlock&nbsp;
Holmes: A Game of Shadows" made a pretty penny,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.720 --> 00:10:03.440
earning $1 billion worldwide on a&nbsp;
combined budget of $215 million.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.080 --> 00:10:09.840
We imagine Warner Brothers was begging for more&nbsp;
sequels, but "The Avengers" broke records in 2012,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.840 --> 00:10:14.640
and Downey went off to make another million&nbsp;
Marvel movies... you know… give or take.

00:10:14.640 --> 00:10:20.480
So here we are, more than a decade later. Does&nbsp;
anyone still care about Sherlock Holmes? The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.480 --> 00:10:25.920
answer is elementary — no. We already know&nbsp;
that this particular incarnation of "Sherlock&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.920 --> 00:10:31.200
Holmes" doesn't have the staying power of an&nbsp;
all-time great franchise like "Star Wars" or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.200 --> 00:10:34.880
"Toy Story." When was the last&nbsp;
time you even thought about it?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.880 --> 00:10:38.720
Our prediction: This'll fall harder&nbsp;
than Sherlock off Reichenbach Falls.

00:10:40.000 --> 00:10:46.240
This may be the Pandora of all hot takes.&nbsp;
After all, "Avatar" is the highest-grossing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.240 --> 00:10:54.480
movie of all time, with $2.8 billion worldwide.&nbsp;
Based on pure math alone, even if "Avatar 2"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.480 --> 00:10:59.600
dropped 50%, it would still be one of the&nbsp;
highest-grossing movies ever with $1.4 billion.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.240 --> 00:11:05.280
Even if it dropped 75%, it'd still be&nbsp;
flirting with $1 billion worldwide.

00:11:05.920 --> 00:11:07.440
"This is why we're here."

00:11:07.440 --> 00:11:10.960
"That's the only reason. It's&nbsp;
what pays for the whole party."

00:11:10.960 --> 00:11:15.280
Given China was still a small market&nbsp;
when the first film was released in 2009,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.280 --> 00:11:20.400
and a re-release in China is the main reason&nbsp;
"Avatar" reclaimed its title from "Avengers:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.400 --> 00:11:26.720
Endgame" in 2020, are we crazy for including&nbsp;
it on our list of movies that will bomb? Maybe.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.720 --> 00:11:33.120
Yet unlike "Star Wars," "Lord of the Rings," or&nbsp;
"The Matrix," "Avatar" didn't have staying power.

00:11:33.120 --> 00:11:39.360
Sure, everybody saw it in 2009 ... but only&nbsp;
because we had to. How many kids dress up as Na'vi&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.360 --> 00:11:46.080
for Halloween? Where are the "Avatar" cartoons,&nbsp;
video games, and toys? Put simply, who cares?

00:11:46.080 --> 00:11:52.880
At this point, "Avatar 2" has been pushed back&nbsp;
six times, will come out 13 years after the first,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.880 --> 00:12:00.880
and reportedly costs $250 million. "Avatar 2"&nbsp;
may make money, but it's hard to see how its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.880 --> 00:12:06.800
box office could be anything but a pale imitation&nbsp;
of its predecessor, and depending on how people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.800 --> 00:12:12.480
react to it, it may pave the way for three more&nbsp;
box office disasters in the series' future.

